{
	"comment": "Shared conformance vectors for the decision an entity's access rules make on one operation: the server's write checks (engine/server/accesswrite.go: txAccess.admits and admitsUpdate) and the client's can() (client/src/access.ts: decideRules) must agree on every case the server can decide. `rules` are an entity's (syncplane.v1.entity).access rules: the operations each names (`write` covers create, update and delete), its source (`requires`, the permissions a caller must hold together, or `admitted: true` for every admitted caller), and its checks (`callerIs`, the field that must name one of the caller's `subjects`, `fields`, the units an update may change, `when`, a name that stands for the rule's condition on the row, and `within`, names that stand for the rule's within entries). `row` is the row the operation reads or deletes, the row a create will store, or the stored row of an update; `final` is the row an update will store, and `changed` the units the update changes, null for an update of fields that no fields list covers. For each row, `held` says whether the caller holds each permission at the row or at a row up its chain of access parents, `fields` gives the value of each field a `callerIs` check reads, `matches` says whether the row matches each condition, and `within` whether the row sits within a row that passes each within entry: the row itself or a row above it (testdata/access/within.json pins that walk). testdata/queryeval pins how a condition evaluates, so these cases name a condition rather than spell one out. A permission missing from `held` is held nowhere. A null in `held`, `fields`, `matches` or `within` is an input the client cannot know, such as a parent row it does not hold; the server always knows every input, so it checks only the cases without a null. A rule admits an operation it names when the caller holds each permission in `requires` and its subject and when checks hold on the row; an update needs one rule whose fields check allows `changed` to admit both `row` and `final`. The answer is \"yes\" when one rule admits the operation, \"no\" when every rule refuses it, and \"unknown\" otherwise. `guards` are the entity's field guards: the `units` each restricts and its `rules`, written as an entity's rules are. A read that names `readFields`, the units it reads, also needs, for each guard that restricts one of them, a rule of that guard that admits reading the row; an update also needs, for each guard that restricts a unit in `changed`, a rule of that guard that admits the update on `row` and on `final`. A guard without a rule for the operation admits it to no caller.",
